d\nREADME.md\nrunbooks/README.md\nprojects/README.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/README.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0008.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0018.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0009.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0019.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0002.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0012.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0006.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0016.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0022.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0007.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0017.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0023.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0003.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0013.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0004.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0014.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0020.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0010.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0001.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0011.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0005.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0015.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0021.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/validate_runbook_generation.py\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/PROJECT_BRIEF.md\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"jq '.assignments[] | select(.id==\\\"R0024\\\" or .id==\\\"R0025\\\" or .id==\\\"R0026\\\")' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json; sed -n '1,220p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/PROJECT_BRIEF.md; sed -n '1,220p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/README.md; sed -n '1,240p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0022.md; sed -n '1,240p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0023.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\njq: error (at projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json:68): Cannot iterate over null (null)\n# Product brief \u2014 Mock Fantasy Quest 4\n\n**Source of truth:** [`INITIAL.md`](INITIAL.md). This normalized brief supports\ndownstream planning; it preserves source scope and records unknowns rather than\nchoosing them.\n\n## Product problem and outcome\n\nCasual adult players need a compact, approachable fantasy RPG that delivers a\ncomplete story without requiring a long commitment or dependable connection.\nThe first useful session is successful when a new player finishes a first\nturn-based battle and understands how to save and resume. The release outcome\nis an original short campaign that a player can complete through exploration,\ndialogue, quests, combat, rewards, saving, and a short ending.\n\n## Category, users, and core experience\n\n| Area | Normalized first-release statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Category | Deliberately small, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG. |\n| Users | Casual and adult players, including people using keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad, captions, adjustable text, and visual settings. |\n| Campaign boundary | One town, one dungeon, and one guided, short ending; no further regions or chapters are required. |\n| Core loop | Explore, converse, accept or advance a quest, enter party-based turn-based combat, manage rewards/inventory, save, and continue. |\n| Experience direction | Original warm hand-painted fantasy, readable silhouettes, and a restrained palette. |\n| Functional reference | *Final Fantasy V* may inform only the high-level scope of a party-based turn-based adventure. |\n\n## Selected target systems\n\n| Target | First-release role | Input model | Delivery constraints | Out-of-scope boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Responsive public web application | Primary browser surface for the complete game. | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad. | Responsive phone/tablet/desktop layout; browser compatibility and performance budget must be approved before implementation. | Native binaries, a separate web-only campaign, and browser-exclusive mechanics. |\n| Progressive web app (PWA) | Installable delivery of the same shared game core. | Same inputs where the browser/device supports them. | Offline assets and save-data behavior; safe update/cache refresh must preserve confirmed player data. | A PWA-exclusive campaign, mandatory continuous connection, or a different ruleset. |\n\nThe product uses a shared core with platform adaptation only where necessary.\nIt should work on widely used devices and under feasible slow-network\nconditions; exact supported browsers, device classes, storage quotas, and\nnetwork floor are open decisions.\n\n## First-release scope and measurable success measures\n\nIncluded scope: playable exploration; one town and one dungeon; narrative,\ndialogue, quests, progression and a short ending; onboarding/tutorial; at least\none tutorialized turn-based battle; inventory and rewards; save/load, resume,\ncheckpoints, data-loss recovery; settings and accessibility; audio/visual\nfeedback; content/level delivery; and crash reporting with player-facing error\nrecovery. The source also names account/identity, multiplayer/player safety,\nlive-service operations, and purchase/entitlement handling, but their release\nstatus remains contingent on the explicit conflict decision below.\n\nThe release is measurable when:\n\n- A new player can finish the tutorial battle, create a save, reload it, and\n  reach the short ending on each supported target.\n- The delivered campaign contains exactly one town, one dungeon, and one short\n  ending; added regions or chapters are not needed for release completion.\n- Both targets meet their stated input role; the PWA is installable and each\n  target has a tested offline, save, update, and error-recovery path.\n- Dialogue, inventory, quest, and progression UI are readable with approved\n  accessibility settings, and captions/visual settings are available where the\n  corresponding feedback exists.\n- An interruption, recoverable error, or safe update offers a player-facing\n  recovery path and does not silently discard the last confirmed save.\n\n## Later roadmap, non-goals, and reference boundary\n\nDeferred roadmap: live events/seasonal content, player-created/shared content\n(levels, mods, designs, stories), advanced analytics, and advanced\nmonetisation. They are excluded from first-release acceptance.\n\nNon-goals: copied branding, assets, text, layouts, characters, story/lore,\naudio, maps, or distinctive interactions; additional towns/dungeons/campaigns;\nnative apps; and platform-specific game variants. No cloud-sync, payment,\nmoderation, service-uptime, or backend commitment is implied before approval.\n\n| Reference boundary | Prohibited | Permitted |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Branding and copy | Names, logos, trademarks, marketing copy, story text, dialogue, characters, and world/lore. | A high-level description of a compact party-based turn-based adventure. |\n| Assets and presentation | Art, music, sound, maps, screen composition, visual language, and layouts. | Original art and UI that fulfil this brief\u2019s own direction. |\n| Interaction design | Reproducing distinctive combat screens, menus, flows, quests, or interaction choreography. | Independently designed mechanics and feedback appropriate to the requested scope. |\n\n## Constraints and safe assumptions\n\nExplicit constraints: web/PWA delivery, offline play, accessibility,\nlocalisation, privacy, safe save/recovery, slow-network feasibility, and\noriginal expression. Safe working assumptions: the offline core does not block\non a remote service; content/UI are original; local persistence is durable\nenough for confirmed saves where browser storage permits; connected features,\nif approved, degrade visibly; and crash diagnostics minimize personal data.\nThese are guardrails, not approval of a stack, vendor, backend, account model,\ndata-retention policy, or technical promise.\n\n## Open decisions requiring human approval\n\n1. **Offline versus connected scope:** Reconcile the offline single-player\n   premise with required account identity, multiplayer safety, live operations,\n   and purchase/entitlement handling. Classify each as release-critical,\n   optional connected enhancement, or deferred.\n2. **Account and data policy:** Decide whether accounts are absent, optional,\n   or mandatory; then define authentication, sync/conflict behavior, retention,\n   deletion, and offline/service-outage recovery.\n3. **Multiplayer, commerce, and safety:** State whether multiplayer or\n   real-money purchases actually ship, plus age rating, regions, moderation,\n   reporting, refunds, store, and applicable legal obligations.\n4. **Technical delivery baseline:** Choose engine/framework, repository and CI\n   constraints, browser/device matrix, performance budget, offline-storage\n   quota, update activation policy, error-reporting provider, and cost model.\n5. **Production content and access:** Approve party size, encounter/quest\n   counts, combat/progression balance, language set, audio scope, and the exact\n   accessibility options before production planning.\n# Canonical specification index\n\nThis directory is the canonical, resumable specification for **mock-fantasy-quest-4**. It consolidates F001\u2013F010 without overriding [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), which remains the source of truth. A later author must cite stable IDs here rather than rediscovering scope.\n\n## Control rules\n\n- **P0** is an original, offline-capable 2D single-player fantasy RPG: one town, one dungeon, a short guided campaign and ending, exploration, dialogue/quests, party turn combat, inventory, local saves/recovery, settings/accessibility, responsive web, and PWA distribution.\n- Target-neutral rules live in `requirements.*`; the web/PWA differences live in `platform-appendix.md`. PWA is a delivery adaptation of the shared game, not a second game.\n- `Final Fantasy V` is a functional scope reference for party-based turn adventure only. It permits no copied expression: names, lore, text, art, music, maps, layouts, UI flows, or interaction choreography.\n- `proposed` requirements and every `GATE-*` are not implementation authorisation. Resolve their linked `DEC-*` record before allocating dependent work.\n\n## Chapter register\n\n| Chapter ID | Document / owner | Depends on | State |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| SPEC-00 | `00-domain-discovery.md` / product discovery | INITIAL | complete |\n| SPEC-01 | `01-product-brief.md` / product | SPEC-00 | complete |\n| SPEC-02 | `02-feature-model.md` / game design | SPEC-00\u201301 | complete |\n| SPEC-03 | `03-technical-contract.md` / technical design | SPEC-00\u201302 | complete |\n| SPEC-04 | `04-experience-contract.md` / experience design | SPEC-00\u201303 | complete |\n| SPEC-04A | `04-asset-direction.md` / visual asset direction | SPEC-04 | complete; P0 asset families allocated by later catalogue expansion |\n| SPEC-04B | `04-audio-direction.md` / audio direction | SPEC-04 | complete; P0 cue map allocated by later catalogue expansion |\n| SPEC-11 | `requirements.md`, `requirements.json` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-00\u201304, planning 05\u201310 | complete |\n| SPEC-12 | `traceability-matrix.md` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-11 | complete; runbook IDs pending F012+ |\n| SPEC-13 | `decision-log.md` / product owner | SPEC-00\u201304 | open decisions recorded |\n| SPEC-14 | `platform-appendix.md` / technical + experience owner | SPEC-11 | complete; thresholds gated |\n| SPEC-15 | `domain-completeness-review.md` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-00\u201304, SPEC-11 | complete; gated domains retained |\n\n## Bounded expansion protocol\n\nExpand one domain chapter at a time, preserving IDs and this register: identify entities/rules, authoring inputs, dependencies, P0 boundary, target adaptations, verification, and open decisions. Proposed detailed chapters are shell/session, campaign content, combat, progression/inventory, persistence/recovery, experience/accessibility, PWA/release, and connected-scope holds. A chapter can move from `planned` to `complete` only when its requirements, trace rows, and decision/risk links exist; it never closes a human gate.\n---\ntask_id: R0022\nsequence: 22\ntitle: Define semantic responsive game-shell and navigation contract\ndependencies: R0010,R0021\nrequirement_ids: REQ-0007,REQ-0009,REQ-0010,NFR-0004\nsource_specifications: specification/04-experience-contract.md#information-architecture-and-navigation,specification/04-experience-contract.md#layout-visual-tokens-and-responsive-behaviour\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-0022\nauthoring_batch: B0009\nfactory_stages: F011,F012,F013\nasset_impact: not_applicable\nasset_ids:\nasset_brief: No assets; semantic shell and layout contract only.\naudio_impact: not_applicable\naudio_ids:\naudio_brief:\naudio_duration_seconds: 0\naudio_loop: not_applicable\naudio_style_version:\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nDefine the semantic, responsive web/PWA game-shell contract around the shared\nsession and game state. It specifies labelled navigation, panel/focus routing,\nviewport profiles, reflow, and non-colour cues without creating a PWA-specific\nUI, final presentation assets, or fixed device-support claim.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nR0010 supplies the shared accessibility foundation and R0021 supplies session\nand checkpoint presentation state. This shell consumes their public states and\nroutes panels to them without changing their APIs. GATE-0002 remains an\nimplementation gate for numeric/browser-device thresholds and GATE-0004\nremains a gate for final content and layout choices.\n\n## Ownership\n\n- `src/app/shell/`, `src/platform/web/layout/`, and `tests/app/shell/`.\n- Semantic navigation, viewport profiles, and panel/focus routing API.\n- Title, main controls, status, temporary panels, dialogs, visible focus, and\n  responsive reflow rules around shared game state.\n\n## Required behaviour\n\nProvide semantic landmarks for title navigation, main game controls, objective\nor status, and dialogs. The game view is the primary workspace; Quest, Bag,\nParty, Save, Settings, Help, privacy/diagnostics, and PWA status are labelled\ntemporary panels with a visible title, close control, and known return target.\nIcons always have adjacent text or an accessible name. Title actions reflect\nR0021 availability rather than promising unavailable save actions.\n\nPanel changes preserve prior selection and scroll position where practical.\nClosing returns focus to the invoking control; a newly opened non-modal panel\nreceives focus at its heading. Blocking dialogs describe consequences and use a\nsafe Cancel default. Browser Back closes a transient panel before any leave\nprompt, and navigation may not silently discard recoverable progress.\n\nDefine narrow, medium, and wide profiles. Narrow layouts use safe-area-aware\nworld presentation, an expandable objective summary, reachable actions, and\nfull-screen panels with sticky close/back controls. Medium layouts retain a\nprimary world with contextual side or bottom panel; wide layouts use a centred\nstage with a collapsible status rail and may use two-column inspection. Resize,\nrotation, zoom, virtual keyboards, and PWA display-mode changes preserve game\nstate and selection where practical; if reflow obscures selection, focus moves\nto the panel heading and announces the change.\n\nKeep visible focus, labels, state text, and non-colour cues available across\nprofiles. Touch-operable controls retain the specified target/separation\ncontract; hover is optional enrichment. Text scaling, contrast, typography,\nmotion, and exact threshold verification remain governed by R0010 and the\nunresolved GATE-0002 rather than being inferred here.\n\n## Web and PWA differences\n\nThe responsive web shell and installed PWA consume the same semantic UI,\nnavigation, session state, and shared campaign rules. PWA display-mode reflow\nuses the same profiles, not a forked UI. Installation, service workers, cache\nrefresh, update activation, and a final support matrix are outside this\ncontract and remain gated.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nDo not produce final art, fonts, UI assets, campaign content, audio, a fixed\nbrowser/device matrix, service-worker behaviour, cache/update implementation,\nor a duplicate PWA interface. Do not resolve GATE-0002 or GATE-0004.\n\n## Asset assessment\n\n`asset_impact` is `not_applicable`: semantic shell and layout rules create no\nvisual/source asset. Do not create or reserve icons, fonts, mockups,\nillustrations, or other product assets; `asset_ids` remains blank.\n\n## Audio assessment\n\n`audio_impact` is `not_applicable`: this shell contract owns no cue selection,\nplayback, or mix. Keep all audio metadata blank/default and create no audio\nasset.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Use representative session and viewport fixtures to prove labelled controls,\n  legal focus order, panel return targets, and safe dialog defaults through\n  resize and panel changes.\n- Prove narrow, medium, and wide profiles retain all required controls without\n  hover-only operation or a PWA-specific UI branch; test reflow focus recovery.\n- Compare metadata, requirements, provenance, dependencies, and ownership to\n  IMP-0022 and the manifest; retain GATE-0002 and GATE-0004 without treating\n  documentation as approval.\n\n## Evidence\n\nRetain semantic-tree/focus traces, viewport and panel fixtures, PWA/web parity\ncomparison, metadata comparison, and the unresolved gate records.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The shell supplies semantic navigation, labelled panels, safe dialog and\n  focus routing, and responsive profiles around shared game state.\n- Web and installed PWA share one UI contract; no PWA-specific interface,\n  support-matrix claim, or lifecycle implementation is introduced.\n- GATE-0002 and GATE-0004 remain explicit implementation gates.\n---\ntask_id: R0023\nsequence: 23\ntitle: Map semantic actions across supported inputs\ndependencies: R0002,R0021,R0022\nrequirement_ids: REQ-0005,REQ-0009,NFR-0004\nsource_specifications: specification/04-experience-contract.md#input-and-interaction-adaptation\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-0023\nauthoring_batch: B0009\nfactory_stages: F011,F012,F013\nasset_impact: not_applicable\nasset_ids:\nasset_brief: No assets; input semantics only.\naudio_impact: not_applicable\naudio_ids:\naudio_brief:\naudio_duration_seconds: 0\naudio_loop: not_applicable\naudio_style_version:\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nDefine one semantic input-action vocabulary for web and PWA adapters, covering\nmovement, inspect/interact, confirm, cancel/pause, panels, navigation, and\nlegal combat action/target selection. It makes equivalent actions available\nacross keyboard, pointer, touch, and detected gamepad without creating\ninput-specific rules or committing to unsupported devices.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nR0002 supplies shared command/revision safety, R0021 supplies safe session and\npause intents, and R0022 supplies semantic controls and focus routing. This\ncontract maps physical input to those existing actions and does not alter game\nrules, persistence, or shell ownership. GATE-0002 remains an implementation\ngate for approved browser/device compatibility claims.\n\n## Ownership\n\n- `src/platform/web/input/`, `src/app/input/`, and `tests/platform/input/`.\n- Input binding and availability state plus the semantic input-action API.\n- Repeat, conflict, disconnect, and fallback handling for supported adapters.\n\n## Required behaviour\n\nUse a single action vocabulary: move, inspect/interact, confirm, cancel/pause,\nopen quest, open bag, open settings, navigate previous/next, and choose a\nlegal combat action/target. Equivalent actions invoke the same command and\nreceive the same state/result feedback. A core journey must not require pointer\nprecision, drag, multi-touch, rapid repetition, or a timeout.\n\nKeyboard supports Arrow keys/WASD where appropriate, Tab/Shift+Tab in visual\nreading order, Enter/Space confirm, Escape cancel/close/pause, and an early\nSkip to game controls route. Pointer clicks operate the semantic controls;\nright-click and hover are optional. Touch controls are labelled and reachable,\nuse explicit tap/target confirmation, and do not require pinch, swipe-only\nnavigation, double tap, drag, or long press.\n\nFeature-detect gamepad input rather than promising a model. When detected,\npair action text with any glyph; map directional navigation, primary confirm,\nsecondary cancel, and menu pause. A disconnect preserves state and supplies\nkeyboard/mouse/touch alternatives without blocking. Focus-only browser input\nuses the semantic focus order when presented, but continuous exploration on\nunapproved targets is not promised. Standard semantic roles, names, states,\nand labelled alternatives expose the same actions to browser assistive input;\nvoice recognition is neither required nor enabled.\n\nRepeat handling must be action-aware. Navigation/movement repeat is bounded by\nthe shared command contract; confirm, destructive choices, legal combat\nactions, and target selection accept one deliberate activation per eligible\nstate/revision. Stale, held, or repeated input may not confirm deletion,\nconsume multiple turns, replay rewards, or bypass a modal Cancel default.\nSurface remap conflicts before saving, while leaving the approved depth of\nremapping unresolved.\n\n## Web and PWA differences\n\nThe same action vocabulary and adapters apply in responsive web and installed\nPWA use. Availability may be reported from feature detection, but neither\nsurface promises a controller model or a final browser/device matrix. Native\nclients, install lifecycle, cache behaviour, and PWA-specific rules are outside\nthis contract.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nDo not implement native clients, voice recognition, arbitrary remapping,\ninput-specific gameplay rules, final support guarantees, UI assets, audio,\nstorage drivers, or product gate approval. Do not resolve GATE-0002.\n\n## Asset assessment\n\n`asset_impact` is `not_applicable`: input semantics needs no product visual or\nsource asset. Do not create or reserve controller glyphs, icons, illustrations,\nor other assets; `asset_ids` remains blank.\n\n## Audio assessment\n\n`audio_impact` is `not_applicable`: this contract selects no cue, playback, or\nmix. Keep audio IDs, brief, duration, loop policy, and style version\nblank/default and create no incidental audio.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Use deterministic adapter fixtures to prove keyboard, pointer, touch, and\n  detected-gamepad events produce equivalent semantic actions and feedback.\n- Prove held/repeated/stale input cannot duplicate destructive confirmation,\n  combat selection, rewards, or commands; verify explicit legal-action and\n  revision checks remain in place.\n- Prove a gamepad disconnect preserves state and exposes a usable\n  keyboard/mouse/touch fallback. Compare metadata, provenance, requirements,\n  dependencies, and ownership to IMP-0023 and the manifest while retaining\n  GATE-0002.\n\n## Evidence\n\nRetain cross-input action traces, repeat-safety and stale-input fixtures,\ndisconnect/fallback output, manifest/catalogue comparison, and the unresolved\nGATE-0002 record.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Keyboard, pointer, touch, and feature-detected gamepad map to equivalent\n  semantic actions with labelled accessible fallbacks.\n- Repeat, stale input, and gamepad disconnect cannot duplicate destructive or\n  turn actions and leave a usable fallback.\n- GATE-0002, remapping depth, and unapproved target compatibility remain\n  explicit and unresolved.\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,140p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.